WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.189
The dates driving this
work come from FedRAMP,

2
00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:06.575
CISA and OMB, and each one
binds a different party.

3
00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:10.421
Every date on the slide was checked
against its issuing document,

4
00:00:10.421 --> 00:00:15.050
and where a document gives a period instead
of a date, the date is counted from it.

5
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:21.138
The nearest one is the post-quantum
migration plan, due October 22, 2026.

6
00:00:21.138 --> 00:00:27.698
Logging plans, CISA's remediation timelines
and FedRAMP's vulnerability rules follow

7
00:00:27.698 --> 00:00:29.495
over the next few months,

8
00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:33.899
and FedRAMP's 2026 rules become
mandatory on January 1, 2027.

9
00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:38.300
Further out, there's
TLS 1.3 support by 2030.

10
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.725
Some things also moved.

11
00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:43.088
Software bills of materials
became a contract choice,

12
00:00:43.088 --> 00:00:46.750
but the software and hardware
inventory is still required.

13
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:48.956
The practical advice is simple:

14
00:00:48.956 --> 00:00:52.688
keep one dated register, and
re-verify it at every gate.

15
00:00:52.688 --> 00:00:54.770
Now, what does Moderate actually mean?
